    <div>
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>Ali Ganjizadeh</h1>
      <p class="dek">I build and evaluate AI for medical imaging, and I keep asking whether the result is real.</p>
    </div>
    <img class="photo" src="/assets/img/ali.jpg" alt="Portrait of Ali Ganjizadeh" width="120" height="120">
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Today I’m a Senior Technical Product Manager at <a href="https://hoppr.ai">HOPPR</a>, which builds foundation models for medical imaging. I work across the whole life of a model: where the data comes from, how it is de-identified, curated and labeled, what the model is asked to predict, and how it is trained, evaluated and deployed. I joined HOPPR as a research scientist in January 2025 and moved into product that September.</p>

    <p>Evaluation is the center of my work. I build the systems that grade a model’s work, including automated graders and LLM-as-judge methods (where one AI model scores another), grounded in how clinicians actually judge a result, and I’ve contributed to published work on measuring medical AI, such as <a href="https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-demos.40">RadEval</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10278-024-01282-9">RIDGE</a>. I’ve also learned not to trust a good score until I know what the model actually saw: I once traced a model’s quiet loss of accuracy to a preprocessing step that flattened the image intensities a radiologist reads.</p>

    <p>Before HOPPR I was a postdoctoral research fellow at the Mayo Clinic Radiology AI &amp; Informatics Lab, where I still collaborate. I built tools that let researchers pull and curate imaging data for themselves, a grant-funded model for detecting prostate cancer recurrence on MRI, and language-model pipelines for extracting data from clinical reports.</p>

    <p>I earned my MD at Tehran University of Medical Sciences, then worked as an emergency physician at Susangerd General Hospital in Khuzestan, Iran. Over the same period I was product manager at Neuronio, where we moved an FDA-cleared dementia-screening product from neurology clinics into primary care and grew its revenue sixfold. I also hold an MBA in data science and business analytics.</p>

    <p>While in medical school I co-founded ventures in health and medical education, including a healthcare product consultancy that ran due diligence for investors, and a public-health school run as a policy simulation, which received the AMEE Patil Innovation in Teaching Award.</p>

    <p>I’m Vice Chair of the AI Education Committee at the Society for Imaging Informatics in Medicine (SIIM), and I help develop AI standards guidelines with Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE), an initiative RSNA co-founded. I enjoy teaching clinicians and students how to judge an AI tool. Away from a screen, I’m usually on a mountain bike.</p>
  </div>

